-- title:
--   Reduced geometric special fibre gives reduced varpi-quotients on affine charts
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring, $\kappa$ a field and $\varphi\colon R\to\kappa$ a ring homomorphism, and let $\varpi\in R$ satisfy $\varphi(\varpi)=0$ and be such that the ideal $(\varpi)=\mathrm{span}\{\varpi\}$ is maximal in $R$. Let $X$ be a scheme and $f\colon X\to\operatorname{Spec}R$ a morphism, and assume that the fibre product of $f$ with $\operatorname{Spec}(\varphi)\colon\operatorname{Spec}\kappa\to\operatorname{Spec}R$ is a reduced scheme. Let $V$ be an open subscheme of $X$ which is an affine open. Then, giving the ring of sections $\Gamma(X,V)$ the $R$-algebra structure coming from the ring homomorphism $R\to\Gamma(X,V)$ obtained by composing the inverse of the canonical isomorphism $R\cong\Gamma(\operatorname{Spec}R,\top)$ with the map $f^{\sharp}$ on sections from the whole of $\operatorname{Spec}R$ to $V$, the quotient ring $\Gamma(X,V)/\bigl(\varpi\,\Gamma(X,V)\bigr)$, i.e. the quotient by the ideal generated by the image of $\varpi$, is reduced.
--
--   This is the statement that reducedness of the geometric special fibre $X\times_{\operatorname{Spec}R}\operatorname{Spec}\kappa$ descends to reducedness of the mod-$\varpi$ sections on each affine chart of $X$, the chart-level input to reducedness arguments for special fibres of integral models. It is used in the study of models of modular curves, where it feeds flatness and freeness statements for sheaves of sections over the base.
